      <h2 id="introduction" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-4">The &#36;2/mo Illusion — Why Your VPS Probably Costs 4x More Than You Think</h2>
      <p>In June 2026, a VPS provider called Godlike.Host ran an ad on LowEndBox promoting a Ryzen 9 VPS for "just &#36;2/mo." The deal looked incredible — 2GB RAM, NVMe storage, and a modern AMD processor for less than the price of a coffee. LowEndBox commenters did what the community does best: they clicked through, read the fine print, and found the catch. The &#36;2/mo price applied to the <strong>first month only</strong>. Renewal was &#36;8/mo — a 300% markup that wasn't disclosed in the headline.</p>
      <p>This isn't an isolated incident. The bait-and-switch pricing pattern is endemic in the budget VPS space. A provider advertises an eye-poppingly low monthly rate, hooks new users who don't read the terms, and then relies on inertia — most people won't migrate after they've set up their server — to collect the much higher renewal fee.</p>
      <p>In this guide, we expose how the bait-and-switch works, compare 6 whitelisted providers on intro vs renewal transparency, and show you a simple formula to calculate the <strong>real</strong> cost of any VPS plan. <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-400">RackNerd</a> stands out in this landscape because their promotional pricing stays close to the renewal rate — no nasty surprises when your first billing cycle ends.</p>

      <h2 id="bait-and-switch-pattern" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-4">The Bait-and-Switch Pattern — How It Works</h2>
      <p>The playbook is nearly universal across budget hosts that rely on deceptive pricing. Here is the anatomy of a bait-and-switch:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>The Hook:</strong> A headline price that is 50-75% below market rate for the advertised specs. Godlike.Host's &#36;2/mo for a Ryzen 9 VPS is the textbook example — any 2GB Ryzen VPS at sustainable pricing costs &#36;5-8/mo.</li>
        <li><strong>The Fine Print:</strong> The low price applies for 1-3 months. Renewal pricing is buried in the terms of service, not on the order page.</li>
        <li><strong>The Inertia Play:</strong> After spending time setting up a server — installing software, configuring backups, migrating data — most users simply pay the higher renewal rather than migrate.</li>
        <li><strong>The Math:</strong> A &#36;2/mo headline that renews at &#36;8/mo means your first year costs (&#36;2 × 1) + (&#36;8 × 11) = &#36;90. That's &#36;7.50/mo effective — not the &#36;2/mo you thought you were getting.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Warning signs to watch for:</strong></p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Pricing advertised as "first month only" or "first 3 months only"</li>
        <li>No renewal price listed anywhere on the checkout page</li>
        <li>"Limited time deal" language combined with mandatory auto-renewal</li>
        <li>Community warnings on LowEndBox or LowEndTalk about renewal spikes</li>
      </ul>
      <p>How do you protect yourself? Always calculate the total cost of ownership (TCO) for 12 months before clicking buy. Check LowEndBox and LowEndTalk for real user experiences — the community is fast to flag any provider with hidden renewal hikes. <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-400">RackNerd</a> avoids this pattern entirely: their promotional pricing is consistently near flat rate across deals, and annual billing means you pay the same price for the full year.</p>

      <h2 id="racknerd" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-4">RackNerd — Consistently Transparent Promotional Pricing</h2>
      <p><a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" class="text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-400">RackNerd</a> has built its reputation on a simple model: annual deals with flat, transparent pricing. You pay for a year upfront, and that's the price — no month-to-month renewal surprises, no hidden escalation clauses.</p>
      <p><strong>How it works:</strong> A typical RackNerd annual deal looks like &#36;17.88/year for a 2GB KVM VPS. That's roughly &#36;1.49/mo. When the year ends, you can renew at a similar promotional rate (typically within 10-20% of the original) or let the service expire. There is no automatic jump to a 3x higher month-to-month rate.</p>
      <p><strong>Specs that matter:</strong></p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Multiple North American datacenters (Los Angeles, Seattle, Dallas, Chicago, New York, Amsterdam, London, and more)</li>
        <li>NVMe SSD storage across all plans</li>
        <li>1Gbps port speed standard</li>
        <li>SolusVM control panel for easy management</li>
